Many people think Load Balancer and Auto Scaling are the same — but they solve different problems in cloud architecture.

==> Load Balancer (Traffic Manager)

A Load Balancer distributes incoming user requests across multiple running servers.
What it does:
Routes traffic evenly to healthy instances
Prevents any single server from getting overloaded
Improves availability & reliability
Example:
1000 users hit your application → Load Balancer shares traffic across 3 servers
It does NOT create or delete servers.



==> Auto Scaling (Capacity Manager)
Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of servers based on demand.
What it does:
Adds servers when traffic increases
Removes servers when traffic decreases
Optimizes cost & performance
Example:
Traffic spikes at night → Auto Scaling adds 2 more servers automatically
It does NOT distribute traffic

 How They Work Together (Real-World Scenario)
 *Auto Scaling adds/removes instances
* Load Balancer routes traffic to those instances

Load Balancer manages traffic
